WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.712
A transfer moves 100 from A-1001 to merchant M-2001.

2
00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:11.417
The two rows live in different ranges, on different machines.

3
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.778
The simplest plan writes each row on its own.

4
00:00:14.778 --> 00:00:19.016
Debit A-1001, crash before the credit, and 100 has vanished.

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.192
So start again.

6
00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:25.549
Each range is copied to three servers by its own Raft group, with its own leader.

7
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:32.353
The transfer takes a timestamp, 100, and a transaction record in range 1, marked pending.

8
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:36.931
Then it writes intents: provisional values that point at that record.

9
00:00:36.931 --> 00:00:39.416
Each range's Raft group copies its own.

10
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.348
To commit, the gateway flips one thing, the record, from pending to committed.

11
00:00:44.348 --> 00:00:45.992
That's a single Raft write.

12
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:50.073
Both intents now count as committed, though nobody touched them.

13
00:00:50.073 --> 00:00:51.599
They're cleaned up later.

14
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.423
Had the gateway crashed before the flip, both intents would be thrown away.

15
00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:57.595
One record decides.

16
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:04.237
Right after the commit, an auditor starts on a server whose clock runs 120 ms slow.

17
00:01:04.237 --> 00:01:06.176
It reads at timestamp 30.

18
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:09.593
It finds M-2001's new value, written at 100.

19
00:01:09.593 --> 00:01:12.087
At 30, that looks like the future.

20
00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:15.240
Skip it, and the audit misses the transfer.

21
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:21.514
But CockroachDB allows clocks to differ by up to 500 milliseconds, the max offset.

22
00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:27.372
So a value written between 30 and 530 may have committed before the read began.

23
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:30.415
The auditor can't skip an uncertain value.

24
00:01:30.415 --> 00:01:35.025
It moves its timestamp past 100 and restarts, and sees the transfer.

25
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.337
Had it met a pending intent, it would wait on the record instead.

26
00:01:39.337 --> 00:01:40.419
It never guesses.

27
00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:48.491
Intents on every range, one replicated record that decides, and readers that wait or restart instead of guessing.
